 \begin{abstract}
-Biocapacity is reported as an aggregate, despite wide differences in yield and regeneration among its constituent land classes. A rising total alone cannot reveal whether its composition has shifted. We examine this accounting problem with a two-land model that includes delayed ecological regeneration, population response, and an explicit projection of the quality state onto an imposed admissible interval. The quality ceiling is a model-imposed admissibility bound, not an estimated ecological threshold. Under the model's baseline conditions, a shortfall-triggered transfer to fast-provisioning land with a higher annual contribution per hectare adds to model biocapacity through the conversion term even as capital-land area falls. In a representative run, total model biocapacity rises $39\%$ as capital-land area falls $46.16\%$; the modeled capital-land contribution falls $51.72\%$. On the reduced fixed-demand comparison, the equality relation reaches the minimum-area floor without a fold or critical slowing; this result does not characterize the separate surplus no-conversion family or every trajectory of the delayed model. Separately, NFA Edition 2026 account values for 1961--2023 show total world biocapacity up $24.86\%$, while $B/P$ falls $52.71\%$ and the production footprint per person rises $16.73\%$. Cropland contributes $+24.06$ percentage points, Forest biocapacity $-5.95$, and built-up land $+7.44$ as an accounting class. These account movements are not measurements of physical stocks. Sharp static bounds quantify how independent area and quality observations narrow the compatible capital-land set. Integrated contribution budgets, checked under timestep refinement, separate conversion from the other active model channels. Component values help interpret the aggregate.
+Biocapacity combines land area and productivity in an aggregate annual account. We examine the information retained by this total using a conserved-area two-land model with delayed ecological regeneration, demographic response and an imposed quality constraint. Conversion increases the model account when the receiving land class has the higher per-hectare contribution, even as ecological-capital land declines. In a representative simulation, biocapacity increases $39\%$, capital-land area decreases approximately $46\%$, and its modeled account contribution decreases $51.72\%$. Sharp static bounds quantify how independent land-area and quality observations restrict the compatible allocation set. Integrated contribution budgets distinguish conversion, maintenance and retirement, restoration, quality and fast-yield effects. In the 100-year fixed-demand simulation, conversion contributes $0.547925$\,gha, while the net account increase is $0.231310$\,gha; other contributions largely offset conversion. Budget closure improves under timestep refinement. The reduced fixed-demand equality relation reaches the minimum-area boundary without a fold or critical slowing; this result is specific to that comparison. Separately, National Footprint Accounts Edition 2026 values for 1961--2023 show world biocapacity increasing $24.86\%$, biocapacity per person decreasing $52.71\%$, and production footprint per person increasing $16.73\%$. Cropland, forest and built-up accounts contribute $+24.06$, $-5.95$ and $+7.44$ percentage points, respectively, to total biocapacity change. These are annual account changes rather than physical-stock observations. The results support reporting constituent accounts alongside independently measured ecological assets.
 
 \end{abstract}
 
